.TH TAM-JOURNAL 1 "2026-11-11" "tam-journal 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
tam-journal \- edit a daily plain text journal or append a quick note
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tam-journal
.RB [ add ]
.RB [ --date
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens a durable private draft in Micro. With no subcommand it edits the day's
journal; add starts a blank note and appends it after saving and closing Micro.
The default day is local today when capture starts. Dates are checked as
Gregorian dates; quick-note timestamps record actual capture date/time and zone.
An unchanged edit or whitespace-only note cancels without creating a day file.
.PP
The journal directory defaults to $HOME/Journal and can be overridden with
TAM_JOURNAL_DIR, which must be absolute. Each day is YYYY-MM-DD.txt, UTF-8 text.
New files have mode 0600, new directories 0700. Existing journal directories
must be owned by the user and private. There is no database or background service.
.SH DATA SAFETY
Micro edits .drafts files; original daily text is replaced atomically only after
the editor succeeds and the new text is valid. Per-day locks serialize commits
and full-day edits. Outside modifications detected during a full-day edit cause
refusal rather than overwrite. A failed or conflicting save retains its draft
and prints the path. Open that file in Micro to recover its text. Files larger
than 16 MiB, symlinks, nonregular files and invalid UTF-8 are refused.
.PP
Interactive editing has no timeout. A crash may leave a draft. A failure after
replacement but before directory sync may have committed the text; inspect the
day before retrying the retained draft to avoid duplicating the note.
Direct edits in another program do not take tam-journal's advisory lock.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0: success or cancellation. 1: editor or data failure. 2: invalid usage.
3: conflicting or unavailable daily lock.
.SH FILES
Journal data and retained drafts belong to the user and are independent of
package upgrades or rollback. No automatic sync, publishing or backup is added.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR micro (1)
